      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's dive into the math.\n\nThe interest portion of your payment is calculated using the formula:\n\nInterest = Principal x Rate x Time\n\nWhere:\n- Principal is the outstanding balance (the amount you still owe)\n- Rate is the interest rate on your loan (expressed as a decimal)\n- Time is the number of months since the loan began\n\nFor example, let's say your loan has a principal balance of $10,000, an interest rate of 6% (0.06 as a decimal), and you've made 12 payments (1 year). The interest for the first month would be:\n\nInterest = $10,000 x 0.06 x 1 = $600\n\nThis means that $600 of your monthly payment goes towards paying off the interest, and the remaining $400 goes towards reducing the principal balance.\n\nNow, as the loan progresses, the interest portion of your payment will decrease, and the principal portion will increase. This is because the principal balance is decreasing, so the interest rate (as a percentage of the principal) is decreasing.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's say you've made 6 months of payments, and the principal balance is now",
